<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Efficiency comparison between PCD and ours measured on real-robot experiments.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.4   Ablation Study</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Effect of Stage 1 vs. Stage 2.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 Real-world Experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Self-Improving VLA Policies: Selected Diffusion Noise for Spurious-Robust Action Smoothing" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> (left) ablates SDN’s two-stage pipeline. SDN-Grounding (Stage 1) mitigates visual hallucinations through contrastive filtering, improving performance by +13.33% on average, while SDN-Smoothness (Stage 2) yields an even larger gain (+16.67%), emphasizing the importance of kinematic feasibility. When combining both stages, we achieve the best performance (48.33% avg.), revealing strong synergy between semantic grounding and motion stability. The largest improvement appears on Cube2Drawer, with a +23.33% gain over the base VLA. We provide additional synergy analyses in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
